---
// src/components/ui/HeroDotPattern.astro
// Purely decorative full-bleed dot-matrix texture for the hero section.
// Inspired by inertiajs.com's hero: a soft dot-grid texture, not a
// literal shape (no clouds/logos).
//
// This is a NEW, separate decorative layer from HeroScatterDecoration.astro
// — that component was built for a light hero background (frosted-glass
// panels tinted with signal colors) and doesn't apply to Hero.astro's
// current solid `bg-electric` dark background. This file reuses only its
// conventions (see below), not its markup or colors.
//
// COLOR: white only, dot color at ~60% opacity — clearly, prominently
// visible against bg-electric (#3B4EFF). (Earlier passes used isolated
// ~10-15% then ~18-24% opacity corner/side clusters, then a full-bleed
// white layer at ~40% opacity, then a full-bleed black layer at ~60%
// opacity; this pass keeps the full-bleed single-layer approach and the
// ~60% opacity but switches the dot color back to white so it reads
// clearly against the dark background.)
// No new color tokens introduced.
//
// ANIMATION: two layered motion effects, both defined entirely in the
// <style> block below (no markup changes, no extra elements — the shine
// sweep uses a `::before` pseudo-element on `.hero-dot-field` rather than
// a sibling `<div>`, since a pseudo-element is sufficient here):
//   1) WAVE SHIFT — `.hero-dot-field`'s own `background-position` is
//      animated diagonally from `0 0` to exactly one tile (`14px 14px`),
//      `linear`/`infinite`, so the dot grid appears to drift continuously
//      with a perfectly seamless loop (the pattern is identical at the
//      start and end of the cycle since the shift is an exact multiple
//      of the `14px 14px` tile size).
//   2) GLASS SHINE SWEEP — a `::before` pseudo-element layered on top of
//      the dots holds an oversized diagonal `linear-gradient` "band"
//      (soft white, low opacity, feathered edges via wide gradient
//      stops). Its `background-position` is animated across a much
//      larger `background-size` so the band sweeps across the element on
//      a loop; because the band reads as fully transparent at both ends
//      of the animation range, the reset-to-start jump is not visible.
//      This layer reuses the *exact same* `mask-image` ellipse as the
//      dots (see <style> below) so the shine sweep is clipped by the
//      same center clearance and never crosses over the content column.
//   Both animations respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` — they are
//   disabled entirely (frozen to a static frame) for users who request
//   reduced motion.
//
// VISIBILITY: like HeroScatterDecoration.astro, the `lg:`-only visibility
// toggle is NOT hardcoded in this file — the consumer (Hero.astro) applies
// `class="hidden lg:block"` on this component's usage, which flows into
// the `class` prop below and lands on the wrapper.
//
// APPROACH (replaces the previous 8-cluster layout): instead of several
// small, individually-masked "blob" clusters positioned in the side
// margins, this is now a SINGLE full-bleed dot layer covering the whole
// `<section>` (all 4 edges), with a SINGLE `mask-image` applied to that
// one layer. The mask is an ellipse centered on the section: opaque
// (dots visible) toward all 4 edges, fading to fully transparent (no
// dots) toward the center — where the centered `max-w-2xl` content
// column (badge/heading/paragraph/buttons) lives. The visual effect is
// dots covering the full section but "pushed away" from the content,
// rather than isolated clusters confined to the side margins.
//
// TEXT-COLUMN CLEARANCE: Hero.astro centers its content in a `max-w-2xl`
// (672px) column with no max-width on the `<section>` itself. At the
// narrowest width this component ever renders (`lg:` = 1024px), that
// column spans roughly 17%-83% of the viewport width, vertically
// centered in the section. The mask's inner ellipse (see <style> below)
// is sized to keep just enough clearance for that column — flat
// 0%-opacity through roughly the center 25% of the section's width and
// a comparable share of its height, intentionally tight rather than
// generous — with the fade-out ramping up from there out to the edges,
// which are fully opaque. This lets the dots creep in much closer to
// the text than a wider clear zone would, without rendering directly
// behind the badge/heading/paragraph/buttons. Widening the viewport
// beyond 1024px only shrinks the column's share of the total width, so
// clearance is preserved (and grows) at every larger breakpoint too.
//
// STACKING ORDER: positioned at `-z-[5]`, matching HeroScatterDecoration's
// documented reasoning — less negative than the `.hero-grain` overlay's
// `-z-[9]` (grain paints furthest back, this dot texture paints just in
// front of it), while still sitting behind the in-flow, un-z-indexed
// content column.
//
// Implementation: CSS `radial-gradient` for the repeating dot grid, with
// a second radial-gradient used as a single section-wide `mask-image` so
// the dots fade out toward the center instead of reading as a hard-edged
// rectangle of dots (or, as in the previous pass, several disconnected
// blobs). No inline `style="..."` attributes anywhere below; the <style>
// block only carries the background-image/mask-image pattern itself,
// which has no Tailwind utility equivalent. Sizing/opacity of the
// wrapper is Tailwind classes; the dot color/spacing and the mask shape
// live in the pattern's <style> block since Tailwind has no utility for
// either.

export interface Props {
  class?: string;
}

const { class: className } = Astro.props;
---

<div
  class:list={['hero-dot-field pointer-events-none absolute -z-[5] inset-0', className]}
  aria-hidden="true"
></div>

<style>
  /* Single full-bleed dot texture, faded out toward the section's center
     (where the content column sits) by one radial mask, so dots are
     visible at all 4 edges but fully transparent behind the content.
     The dot layer's own background-position is animated diagonally by
     exactly one tile (14px 14px) for a seamless drift loop. */
  .hero-dot-field {
    position: relative;
    background-image: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6) 1px, transparent 1.6px);
    background-size: 14px 14px;
    background-position: 0 0;
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 75% 75% at 50% 50%, transparent 0%, transparent 25%, #000 85%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 75% 75% at 50% 50%, transparent 0%, transparent 25%, #000 85%);
    animation: hero-dot-drift 4s linear infinite;
  }

  /* Glass shine sweep: a soft, feathered diagonal highlight band layered
     on top of the dots via ::before. The band lives on an oversized
     background-size and is swept across by animating background-position,
     so it reads as fully transparent at both ends of the loop and the
     reset jump is invisible. Reuses the exact same radial ellipse mask
     as the dot layer so the sweep is clipped to the same center
     clearance and never crosses the content column. */
  .hero-dot-field::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      115deg,
      transparent 30%,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45) 50%,
      transparent 70%
    );
    background-size: 300% 300%;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 75% 75% at 50% 50%, transparent 0%, transparent 25%, #000 85%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 75% 75% at 50% 50%, transparent 0%, transparent 25%, #000 85%);
    animation: hero-dot-shine 3s linear infinite;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  @keyframes hero-dot-drift {
    from {
      background-position: 0 0;
    }
    to {
      background-position: 14px 14px;
    }
  }

  @keyframes hero-dot-shine {
    0% {
      background-position: -100% -100%;
    }
    100% {
      background-position: 200% 200%;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero-dot-field {
      animation: none;
      background-position: 0 0;
    }

    .hero-dot-field::before {
      animation: none;
      background-position: -100% -100%;
    }
  }
</style>
